@lupaglong345 
Ah ok, kaya ko sinabi na 6 years course (it depends naman kung anong course).
Kung abogado ka 6 years course mo, kung civil engineering ka like sa akin 5 years course, pero Kong criminology lang ay 4 year's course lang.

@napier7290 I understand your problems and we all do. I definitely see that there are gaping holes that needs to be resolved in the K to 12 program. Even if we already have years in this program, we are still definitely at the start and the program has a lot to work on. Yes, you can say that there are no groundbreaking stuffs that you will learn in those two years, but I can definitely say to you that there are no real groundbreaking stuffs if there is no creativity, it is people, the students who will discover new things that are found in already existing and mundane stuffs.

You just can't rely on the teachers what you can learn because what you can learn in those two years are crucial, and if you could only look at a different perspective, you'll see that despite the negatives, it has some undeniable positive impact on those who undergo it.

You're also right that we're experiencing another major recession with the rising fuel prices, but those are beyond our control. We can't stop wars and conflicts, and those will definitely affect the world market that will go spiral into stagflation. If we were to compromise the system of education, do you think it will do good to solve this things?

@darkmatter515 K-12 IS ONLY A BURDEN FOR THOSE STUDENTS WHO NEEDS TO PURSUE COLLEGE AS FAST AS POSSIBLE, IF THE JUNIOR HIGH SCHOOL TEACHERS  ONLY GIVES PROPER AND RIGHT KNOWLEDGE TO THE STUDENTS AND PRACTICE THEM BEFOREE ENTERING COLLEGE, THEN WE DONT NEED THE K-12 ANYMORE

